{"schema_version":1,"snapshot":"2021-12-03","source":{"repository":"matthiaskoenig/pkdb_analysis","path":"tests/data/testdata_concise_false.zip","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/matthiaskoenig/pkdb_analysis/develop/tests/data/testdata_concise_false.zip"},"sid":"PKDB00491","namespace":"pkdb","name":"Trang1985","licence":"closed","access":"public","date":"2021-07-08","creator":"adriankl","curators":["adriankl","mkoenig"],"substances":["water","caffeine (137X)"],"identifiers":{"source_study_sid":"PKDB00491","pmid":"3986300","pmcid":null,"doi":null,"pkdb_sid":"PKDB00491"},"reference":{"title":"Relationship between total body clearance of caffeine and urine flow rate in elderly men.","date":"1985-06-18","journal":"Biopharmaceutics & drug disposition","authors":["J M Trang","J Blanchard","K A Conrad","G G Harrison"],"abstract":"The total body clearance (CL), renal clearance (CLR), and nonrenal clearance (CLNR) of caffeine from plasma were determined following the intravenous administration of caffeine (4 mg kg-1) to ten healthy men (aged 66-86 years) on three separate occasions. Positive correlations were observed between CL and urine flow rate (UFR), between CLR and UFR, and between CLNR and UFR (r = 0.8947, p = 0.0002; r = 0.8832, p = 0.0003; and r = 0.8920, p = 0.0002, respectively). Previous studies have established similar relationships between CLR and UFR for caffeine and its initial dimethylxanthine metabolites; theophylline, theobromine, and paraxanthine. A relationship between CL and UFR has not been reported previously."},"row_counts":{"groups":6,"individuals":60,"interventions":1,"outputs":40,"timecourses":0,"scatters":3},"entity_counts":{"groups":1,"individuals":10,"interventions":1,"outputs":40,"timecourses":0,"scatters":3},"groups":[{"study_name":"Trang1985","measurement_type":"species","group_count":"10","group_name":"all","max":null,"substance":null,"count":10,"calculation_type":"sample mean","group_parent_pk":null,"sd":null,"unit":null,"se":null,"